Sure! If you already have your HTML `<ul>`/`<ol>`, `<div>`, etc., stored in a JavaScript variable called **`listElement`**, you can pull out every child node whose **`data‑active="true"`** using the helper function I’ve built for you – `getActiveDataEntries`.

Below is a quick rundown:

| Step | What to Do |
|------|------------|
| 1️⃣ | Make sure `listElement` contains the DOM element (or an array of elements) you’re interested in. |
| 2️⃣ | Call `getActiveDataEntries({ listElement })`. By default it looks for the attribute `"data-active"` equal to boolean `true`. |
| 3️⃣ | Inspect the returned result – it’s an array of the matching nodes (or their serialized representations). |

---

### Example Usage

```js
// Suppose this is your list element:
const listElement = document.querySelector('#my-list');   // <ul id="my-list">...</ul>

// Now fetch all children where data-active === true
const activeItems = getActiveDataEntries({
    listElement,
    // You can omit the next two lines unless you want custom values
    // attribute: 'data-active',
    // value: true
});

console.log(activeItems);   // → Array of matched items
```

If you prefer to work directly inside the browser console without the wrapper function, here’s vanilla‑JS equivalent logic:

```js
function findActiveChildren(listEl) {
  return [...listEl.children].filter(child =>
      child.dataset.active && child.dataset.active.toLowerCase() === 'true'
  );
}

const activeItems = findActiveChildren(document.getElementById('my-list'));
console.log(activeItems);
```

---

#### Customizing the Attribute / Value

Sometimes you may want to look for another flag, e.g.:

```js
const flaggedItems = getActiveDataEntries({
    listElement,
    attribute: 'data-selected',   // Look at “data-selected” instead
    value: false                  // …and grab those set to false
});
```

Just adjust the optional parameters accordingly.

Let me know if you’d like a deeper dive into handling nested lists, converting results to plain objects, or anything else!
